1
00:00:02,437 --> 00:00:04,221
Matilda Cruz was 23.

2
00:00:04,308 --> 00:00:05,918
She'd been missing for two weeks

3
00:00:06,006 --> 00:00:09,357
when her body was found
at a county waste facility.

4
00:00:09,444 --> 00:00:11,837
We believe that Matilda
met her killer online

5
00:00:11,924 --> 00:00:14,884
in a chat room dedicated
to lovers of Shakespeare.

6
00:00:14,971 --> 00:00:17,365
We would ask anybody
who uses that chat room

7
00:00:17,452 --> 00:00:20,585
or similar sites
to please contact the CBI

8
00:00:20,672 --> 00:00:23,980
if they come across
any suspicious activity online.

9
00:00:24,067 --> 00:00:27,114
And now Gary Philo,
CEO Of Phiz Systems

10
00:00:27,201 --> 00:00:30,247
and Matilda's boss,
would like to say a few words.

11
00:00:31,727 --> 00:00:33,120
[camera shutters clicking]

12
00:00:33,207 --> 00:00:35,557
Thank you.

13
00:00:35,644 --> 00:00:37,472
I'll be brief.

14
00:00:37,559 --> 00:00:42,303
Matilda Cruz
was a beautiful young soul..

15
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:46,916
...and a treasured member
of our family here at Phiz.

16
00:00:47,003 --> 00:00:50,659
And that is why
I am offering $10 million..

17
00:00:50,746 --> 00:00:54,315
...for information leading
to the arrest of her killer.

18
00:00:54,402 --> 00:00:57,796
Matilda deserves justice,
and she will get it.

19
00:00:57,883 --> 00:00:58,841
Questions?

20
00:00:58,928 --> 00:01:00,147
Wainright needs us.

21
00:01:00,234 --> 00:01:01,757
Super urgent.
He won't say why.

22
00:01:01,844 --> 00:01:03,237
Interesting,
but I can't go yet.

23
00:01:03,324 --> 00:01:04,325
It's a press conference.
Let's go.

24
00:01:04,412 --> 00:01:05,630
I need one minute.

25
00:01:05,717 --> 00:01:08,111
Naturally, we are
all deeply upset

26
00:01:08,198 --> 00:01:09,982
'and saddened at this tragedy.'

27
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:11,941
We will get through this.

28
00:01:12,028 --> 00:01:15,075
'We will come back
stronger than ever.'

29
00:01:15,162 --> 00:01:17,338
For Matilda's sake.

30
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:19,949
Mr. Philo uh, Gary,

31
00:01:20,036 --> 00:01:23,735
what was your personal
impression of Matilda Cruz?

32
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:25,346
[clears throat]

33
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:28,349
Well, as with all our employees

34
00:01:28,436 --> 00:01:31,743
she and I had a nice chat
when she first joined us

35
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:34,311
but beyond that, I never
had the privilege

36
00:01:34,398 --> 00:01:36,052
of getting to
know her personally.

37
00:01:36,139 --> 00:01:38,141
- Come on. Let's go.
- I have 30 more seconds.

38
00:01:38,228 --> 00:01:41,101
Sir, you're a-a famously
thrifty man, aren't you?

39
00:01:41,188 --> 00:01:45,844
Uh, the no-brand sneakers
the compact cars, all that.

40
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:49,892
Excuse me, I don't mean
to be authoritarian--

41
00:01:49,979 --> 00:01:52,329
Then don't be. We're
investigating a murder here.

42
00:01:52,416 --> 00:01:54,592
Are you thrifty or not?

43
00:01:54,679 --> 00:01:58,205
What in the devil does my
thrift have to do with...that?

44
00:01:58,292 --> 00:02:00,294
Ah. You notice
that tiny little beat

45
00:02:00,381 --> 00:02:01,947
you took before you said "that"?

46
00:02:02,034 --> 00:02:04,646
That, my friend,
was a solid-gold tell.

47
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:07,518
- What?
- Never mind. You're thrifty.

48
00:02:07,605 --> 00:02:09,607
You're a legendary
cheapskate, in fact.

49
00:02:09,694 --> 00:02:13,133
And yet, you put up $10 million
of your own precious money

50
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:15,570
to find Matilda's killer.

51
00:02:15,657 --> 00:02:16,919
Why would you do that?

52
00:02:17,006 --> 00:02:19,356
Why?
Because you know for sure

53
00:02:19,443 --> 00:02:21,793
you'll never pay
one single penny of that money

54
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:23,186
because it's you.

55
00:02:23,273 --> 00:02:24,622
You're Matilda's killer.

56
00:02:24,709 --> 00:02:27,190
Ooh! Aah!

57
00:02:27,277 --> 00:02:30,846
Uh, that's assault.
Lisbon, arrest him!

58
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:32,195
Stay there.

59
00:02:32,282 --> 00:02:34,806
[theme music]

60
00:02:44,686 --> 00:02:47,123
Ah. Thank you for
getting in here so promptly.

61
00:02:47,210 --> 00:02:49,343
The FBI needs our help.
Agent Darcy.

62
00:02:49,430 --> 00:02:51,040
We just caught a homicide

63
00:02:51,127 --> 00:02:53,564
where the family of the
victim has statehouse juice.

64
00:02:53,651 --> 00:02:56,045
The lieutenant governor has
specifically requested you

65
00:02:56,132 --> 00:02:58,352
for the task force.

66
00:02:58,439 --> 00:03:00,049
He wants the best
on this one, he says.

67
00:03:00,136 --> 00:03:01,703
Whoa. Well, I'm flattered.

68
00:03:01,790 --> 00:03:03,618
Uh, but we already have a case.
A big case.

69
00:03:03,705 --> 00:03:05,794
Yes. So I've heard.

70
00:03:07,230 --> 00:03:09,798
We don't need the team.
Just Jane.

71
00:03:09,885 --> 00:03:11,539
That is, of course,
if you're willing.

72
00:03:11,626 --> 00:03:13,889
Well, I'm...happy to help

73
00:03:13,976 --> 00:03:16,239
I just-as long as
it's okay with you.

74
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:17,545
'Cause last we spoke,
we weren't exactly

75
00:03:17,632 --> 00:03:19,329
seeing things eye to eye.

76
00:03:19,416 --> 00:03:20,678
The Red John case, you mean.

77
00:03:20,765 --> 00:03:22,724
Yes, it is a mystery, isn't it?

78
00:03:22,811 --> 00:03:24,465
It's not surprising
we have different takes

79
00:03:24,552 --> 00:03:27,903
but that doesn't mean we can't
work together, does it?

80
00:03:27,990 --> 00:03:29,905
Excellent.
It's all settled then.

81
00:03:33,038 --> 00:03:34,997
I know what you're gonna say.
You're gonna warn me

82
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:37,304
that Agent Darcy's
playing games with me.

83
00:03:37,391 --> 00:03:40,045
She's trying to investigate me
on the sly, bless her heart.

84
00:03:40,132 --> 00:03:41,699
Well, yes, exactly.

85
00:03:41,786 --> 00:03:43,353
Well, I play games
better than she does

86
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:44,528
so I'm looking forward to it.

87
00:03:44,615 --> 00:03:46,487
Oh, so you get to go
off and have fun

88
00:03:46,574 --> 00:03:48,967
while we deal with
the Philo mess you've left us?

89
00:03:49,054 --> 00:03:51,709
- I'm pretty sure he's guilty.
- Oh, thanks a lot.

90
00:03:51,796 --> 00:03:53,494
I'd love to stick around
and help you solve this crime

91
00:03:53,581 --> 00:03:55,844
but I can't really
say "no" to the FBI.

92
00:03:55,931 --> 00:03:56,932
You know, Darcy
would leave you alone

93
00:03:57,019 --> 00:03:58,238
if you just told
her the truth.

94
00:03:58,325 --> 00:03:59,587
Which truth
you are talking about?

95
00:03:59,674 --> 00:04:00,849
That you didn't kill Red John

96
00:04:00,936 --> 00:04:01,980
that you lied about it

97
00:04:02,067 --> 00:04:03,243
that you did a lot of stupid

98
00:04:03,330 --> 00:04:05,157
things to cover up the lies

99
00:04:05,245 --> 00:04:08,073
and that now he's back, and
you're very sorry, that truth.

100
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,510
- Eh.
- Can't you see.

101
00:04:09,597 --> 00:04:11,076
Your stubborn arrogance is gonna

102
00:04:11,163 --> 00:04:13,514
bite you in the...Agent Darcy.

103
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:14,993
We gotta get rolling,
they're keeping

104
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,560
the crime scene intact
until we get there.

105
00:04:16,647 --> 00:04:17,953
Okay. I'm ready.

106
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:19,433
Thanks for lending him to me.

107
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:22,392
My pleasure.
Glad to see the back of him.

108
00:04:22,479 --> 00:04:23,872
Oh, okay.

109
00:04:23,959 --> 00:04:26,091
- Well, bye.
- Bye. Just go and have fun.

110
00:04:26,178 --> 00:04:29,747
I'm gonna have a great time.
There's my back. See you.

111
00:04:33,098 --> 00:04:34,230
[sighs]

112
00:04:34,317 --> 00:04:35,536
Hey, boss.

113
00:04:35,623 --> 00:04:38,190
Philo's lawyer's arrived
he wants to talk.

114
00:04:38,278 --> 00:04:39,975
What are you gonna do?

115
00:04:40,062 --> 00:04:42,194
Probably gonna
have to let him go.

116
00:04:42,282 --> 00:04:43,370
[sighs]

117
00:04:46,068 --> 00:04:47,548
Ah.

118
00:04:47,635 --> 00:04:49,158
Here she is.

119
00:04:49,245 --> 00:04:51,595
This is the fool
that arrested me.

120
00:04:51,682 --> 00:04:53,380
I am sorry, sir. I under--

121
00:04:53,467 --> 00:04:56,252
I want this little bitch
toasted like a marshmallow.

122
00:04:56,339 --> 00:04:57,993
You'll excuse
my client's anger.

123
00:04:58,080 --> 00:04:59,777
He has every reason
to feel aggrieved.

124
00:04:59,864 --> 00:05:01,692
'His arrest was
an outrageous abuse of power'

125
00:05:01,779 --> 00:05:04,173
I will be brief, counselor..

126
00:05:04,260 --> 00:05:05,783
...You committed an assault.

127
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,438
We can hold you for 48 hours
without charging you

128
00:05:08,525 --> 00:05:10,310
and we will hold you
for those 48 hours.

129
00:05:11,441 --> 00:05:12,790
Unless, of course you would like

130
00:05:12,877 --> 00:05:15,445
to confess
to the murder of Matilda Cruz

131
00:05:15,532 --> 00:05:17,317
in which case, things would
move a lot more quickly.

132
00:05:17,404 --> 00:05:18,666
Agent Lisbon, this is no way

133
00:05:18,753 --> 00:05:19,710
to treat a man
of Mr. Philo's stand--

134
00:05:19,797 --> 00:05:22,583
'Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.'

135
00:05:22,670 --> 00:05:23,714
She can legally
hold me that long?

136
00:05:23,801 --> 00:05:25,542
Yes, but that doesn't mean--

137
00:05:25,629 --> 00:05:28,458
Don't waste your breath.

138
00:05:28,545 --> 00:05:30,765
[sighs]
You may go.

139
00:05:30,852 --> 00:05:32,114
What?

140
00:05:32,201 --> 00:05:33,550
I'm not gonna pay you $1,000

141
00:05:33,637 --> 00:05:35,900
to hold my hand
while this idiotic girl

142
00:05:35,987 --> 00:05:38,816
acts out her hatred of powerful
father figures. Just go!

143
00:05:41,297 --> 00:05:43,343
As you wish, Mr. Philo.

144
00:05:44,996 --> 00:05:47,085
Yeah.

145
00:05:48,086 --> 00:05:50,437
[telephone ringing]

146
00:06:01,448 --> 00:06:03,885
That creep is a
psychopath, and he's guilty

147
00:06:03,972 --> 00:06:05,452
and we are gonna
make him say so.

148
00:06:05,539 --> 00:06:07,497
He cracked once already
when he hit Jane.

149
00:06:07,584 --> 00:06:09,151
Let's make him crack again.

150
00:06:10,021 --> 00:06:11,458
Okay.

151
00:06:19,291 --> 00:06:20,989
[Darcy]
'Victim was found
early this morning'

152
00:06:21,076 --> 00:06:22,860
'by state park rangers.'

153
00:06:22,947 --> 00:06:27,169
'Victim's name is Kati Bauer.
27, married, one child.'

154
00:06:28,866 --> 00:06:31,303
Multiple stab wounds.
Uh, evidence of sexual assault.

155
00:06:33,262 --> 00:06:36,134
Lieutenant Franklin, is this
exactly how you found the body?

156
00:06:36,221 --> 00:06:38,572
[sighs]
Yes, ma'am. I..

157
00:06:38,659 --> 00:06:41,444
...I-I noticed her car
parked off the road.

158
00:06:41,531 --> 00:06:44,099
It looked like it had
been there overnight, so..

159
00:06:44,186 --> 00:06:47,668
...so I and a couple of my men
took a look around and..

160
00:06:47,755 --> 00:06:49,974
...found the poor woman here.

161
00:06:50,061 --> 00:06:51,846
She looks peaceful.

162
00:06:53,238 --> 00:06:55,153
No signs of struggle.

163
00:07:04,685 --> 00:07:06,513
No defense wounds.

164
00:07:12,606 --> 00:07:13,955
Do you know her?

165
00:07:14,042 --> 00:07:15,609
Was she a local girl?

166
00:07:15,696 --> 00:07:19,526
Uh, Santa Carla,
in the valley, but, uh, no.

167
00:07:20,657 --> 00:07:22,920
No, I didn't know her.

168
00:07:23,834 --> 00:07:25,314
This..

169
00:07:25,401 --> 00:07:29,144
...you see, this
is my first homicide.

170
00:07:29,231 --> 00:07:31,407
Just, it's-it's sad.

171
00:07:31,494 --> 00:07:32,452
Mm.

172
00:07:46,727 --> 00:07:47,641
Wine.

173
00:07:48,946 --> 00:07:49,860
[female #1]
'Wine?'

174
00:07:49,947 --> 00:07:51,383
Wine?

175
00:07:51,471 --> 00:07:53,560
Why is that...Kati, would
drink wine on occasion.

176
00:07:53,647 --> 00:07:54,604
Why is that relevant?

177
00:07:54,691 --> 00:07:56,258
Well, it's probably not.

178
00:07:56,345 --> 00:07:58,086
Um, I'm sorry.

179
00:07:58,173 --> 00:08:01,437
I wasn't listening earlier.

180
00:08:01,524 --> 00:08:04,179
You're her in-laws,
you're not her parents

181
00:08:04,266 --> 00:08:06,355
and this is...
this isn't your house?

182
00:08:06,442 --> 00:08:09,619
We live in Pasadena.
This is Sandy and Kati's house.

183
00:08:09,706 --> 00:08:11,578
Sandy is our son,
her husband.

184
00:08:11,665 --> 00:08:15,103
He's on his way home now
from our New York offices.

185
00:08:15,190 --> 00:08:16,713
When did you last see Kati?

186
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,368
Yesterday morning at 9:00 a.m.

187
00:08:19,455 --> 00:08:22,676
We came to take Megan to our
house for the day and give Kati

188
00:08:22,763 --> 00:08:24,155
a little "me" time.

189
00:08:24,242 --> 00:08:27,768
It's a regular thing
we do.

190
00:08:27,855 --> 00:08:31,336
What does Kati like to do
during her "me" time?

191
00:08:31,423 --> 00:08:32,381
'You know,
I think she just likes'

192
00:08:32,468 --> 00:08:33,861
'to give the help the day off'

193
00:08:33,948 --> 00:08:36,124
and enjoys the solitude
and the peace and quiet.

194
00:08:38,213 --> 00:08:40,781
[Darcy]
'And when did you return here?'

195
00:08:40,868 --> 00:08:43,827
Well, it's all a blur, but it
must have been just before 5:00

196
00:08:43,914 --> 00:08:47,614
because I...do my insulin
shot regularly at that time

197
00:08:47,701 --> 00:08:52,532
and I had to hurry
in order to...do it.

198
00:08:52,619 --> 00:08:54,446
And she was gone.

199
00:08:54,534 --> 00:08:56,927
[beeps]

200
00:08:58,712 --> 00:09:01,453
We waited for two hours
and then we called the police.

201
00:09:06,067 --> 00:09:08,852
Did Kati like to
go up to the mountains a lot?

202
00:09:08,939 --> 00:09:10,375
No.

203
00:09:10,462 --> 00:09:12,900
I mean,
perhaps with Sandy, but..

204
00:09:12,987 --> 00:09:15,859
'...on her own, on a whim, no.'

205
00:09:15,946 --> 00:09:18,427
Kati was very much
a creature of habit.

206
00:09:19,297 --> 00:09:21,473
[cell phone rings]

207
00:09:21,561 --> 00:09:23,475
Oh. It's the house phone.
That's odd.

208
00:09:23,563 --> 00:09:27,044
Sorry. That's-that's me.
I just, uh...I like to fiddle.

209
00:09:28,089 --> 00:09:30,482
Don't mind me. Carry on.

210
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:35,662
- So, Mrs. Bauer, you were say--
- '"New York offices," huh?'

211
00:09:35,749 --> 00:09:38,360
What business
are you and your son in?

212
00:09:38,447 --> 00:09:41,363
We are a design consultancy
for the financial--

213
00:09:41,450 --> 00:09:43,670
Hi. Uh, what kind of
place do you have there?

214
00:09:44,888 --> 00:09:45,976
Uh-huh.

215
00:09:46,063 --> 00:09:47,978
Ah. Oh, interesting. Okay.

216
00:09:49,197 --> 00:09:51,025
No, no, no, no.
Thank you. Bye.

217
00:09:51,112 --> 00:09:52,853
[beep]

218
00:09:54,681 --> 00:09:55,856
Kitchen.

219
00:10:00,164 --> 00:10:02,558
[instrumental music]

220
00:10:26,408 --> 00:10:28,802
[music continues]

221
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:36,418
Only two outgoing calls
on the landline yesterday

222
00:10:36,505 --> 00:10:38,855
one to you, Shirley--
may I call you "Shirley"?

223
00:10:38,942 --> 00:10:41,336
'At around 8:15 in the morning'

224
00:10:41,423 --> 00:10:45,732
and one call to
Mama Sylvia's pizzeria, 12:07.

225
00:10:45,819 --> 00:10:48,778
Which is strange 'cause
there's no pizza box here.

226
00:10:48,865 --> 00:10:50,475
Well, maybe it was a misdial.

227
00:10:50,562 --> 00:10:52,739
Of course.

228
00:10:52,826 --> 00:10:55,002
Probably is that.

229
00:10:55,089 --> 00:10:57,787
A misdial.

230
00:10:57,874 --> 00:10:58,832
[door closes]

231
00:10:58,919 --> 00:11:00,355
[Male #1]
'Hello?'

232
00:11:00,442 --> 00:11:01,399
Oh.

233
00:11:01,486 --> 00:11:04,489
Mom? Dad?

234
00:11:04,576 --> 00:11:07,754
Sandy. Nice to meet you.
I'm very sorry for your loss.

235
00:11:09,843 --> 00:11:12,193
Oh, darling. Oh.

236
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:15,065
These people are here
to investigate the, um..

237
00:11:15,152 --> 00:11:16,240
'...the murder.'

238
00:11:16,327 --> 00:11:18,721
Graciella's with Megan.
She's fine.

239
00:11:18,808 --> 00:11:21,202
Oh.

240
00:11:24,292 --> 00:11:26,337
I can't believe
this is happening to me.

241
00:11:26,424 --> 00:11:29,645
Mr. Bauer,
I'm Agent Darcy, FBI.

242
00:11:29,732 --> 00:11:30,951
I'm gonna have to
ask you a couple questions

243
00:11:31,038 --> 00:11:32,256
if you don't mind.

244
00:11:32,343 --> 00:11:34,824
Did you just get
in from New York?

245
00:11:34,911 --> 00:11:36,130
Yes.

246
00:11:36,217 --> 00:11:38,306
Do you recall
your flight number?

247
00:11:38,393 --> 00:11:41,526
We have a jet...
well, a fractional share.

248
00:11:41,613 --> 00:11:43,659
Sweet.

249
00:11:43,746 --> 00:11:44,965
What time did you land?

250
00:11:45,052 --> 00:11:46,793
Thirty-five minutes ago.

251
00:11:46,880 --> 00:11:49,621
Mr. Bauer, we have to
explore all possibilities.

252
00:11:49,709 --> 00:11:51,885
Sandy, how long have you
and Kati been married?

253
00:11:52,755 --> 00:11:54,496
Uh..

254
00:11:54,583 --> 00:11:55,976
...three years, near enough.

255
00:11:56,063 --> 00:11:58,413
Okay. Tell me about her.

256
00:12:00,284 --> 00:12:02,547
I don't know.

257
00:12:02,634 --> 00:12:04,593
What can you say
about a person?

258
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,204
Show them the clip
on the phone, the song.

259
00:12:12,122 --> 00:12:16,170
♪ That horse
and bull falls over ♪

260
00:12:16,257 --> 00:12:18,346
[Shirley]
'That's Kati.
She was an angel.'

261
00:12:18,433 --> 00:12:22,654
♪ Mama's gonna buy you
a dog named Rover ♪

262
00:12:22,742 --> 00:12:27,094
♪ And if that dog
named Rover won't bark ♪

263
00:12:27,181 --> 00:12:31,011
♪ Mama's gonna buy you
a horse and cart ♪

264
00:12:32,577 --> 00:12:34,231
♪ and if that horse ♪♪

265
00:12:34,318 --> 00:12:36,320
Okay, thanks,
yeah, he's a possibility

266
00:12:36,407 --> 00:12:39,410
but can he overcome his natural
passivity to commit murder?

267
00:12:39,497 --> 00:12:41,021
I don't know.

268
00:12:41,108 --> 00:12:43,588
We have some other
important leads to follow.

269
00:12:43,675 --> 00:12:46,156
Sir, your manner
is deeply inappropriate.

270
00:12:46,243 --> 00:12:47,592
I assure you, Mr. Bauer

271
00:12:47,679 --> 00:12:49,203
- This won't be how we'll--
- Yes, yes.

272
00:12:49,290 --> 00:12:51,640
Uh, Franklin, will you
smooth things over here?

273
00:12:51,727 --> 00:12:53,076
Uh, Darcy and I need to go.

274
00:12:59,648 --> 00:13:01,389
If I may just say
so I'm so very deeply

275
00:13:01,476 --> 00:13:05,959
sorry for your loss,
and I, uh...sorry.

276
00:13:06,046 --> 00:13:07,612
If there's anything
I can do for you all

277
00:13:07,699 --> 00:13:08,875
please don't hesitate to ask.

278
00:13:10,746 --> 00:13:12,052
[indistinct conversations]

279
00:13:12,139 --> 00:13:13,705
Alright,people.
What have we got?

280
00:13:13,793 --> 00:13:15,142
ME says petechial hemorrhaging

281
00:13:15,229 --> 00:13:16,796
indicates death by asphyxia.

282
00:13:16,883 --> 00:13:18,362
The hyoid's intact,
so we're probably looking

283
00:13:18,449 --> 00:13:20,495
at a smothering
rather than a strangulation.

284
00:13:20,582 --> 00:13:22,236
'That's all she wrote.
Blood work's clean.'

285
00:13:22,323 --> 00:13:23,933
Decayed state of the body
means there's no way

286
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,196
to get any useful DNA.
No crime scene, no forensics.

287
00:13:26,283 --> 00:13:27,937
Matilda's unknown
chat room buddy

288
00:13:28,024 --> 00:13:30,244
was definitely the last person
to communicate with her.

289
00:13:30,331 --> 00:13:31,941
I followed
the internet trail he left.

290
00:13:32,028 --> 00:13:33,116
It bounces around
the globe then dead-ends

291
00:13:33,203 --> 00:13:34,944
at a pirate server in Macao.

292
00:13:35,031 --> 00:13:36,250
There's no way
to trace our mystery

293
00:13:36,337 --> 00:13:38,339
Shakespeare fan any further.

294
00:13:38,426 --> 00:13:41,124
Matilda's colleagues at Phiz
didn't know very much about her.

295
00:13:41,211 --> 00:13:44,258
Her close friends and family are
all back east in Philadelphia.

296
00:13:44,345 --> 00:13:46,173
She never spoke about
her online life or dating.

297
00:13:46,260 --> 00:13:47,522
Just family and work stuff.

298
00:13:47,609 --> 00:13:49,263
So we've got zip.

299
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:51,700
If we can't make
a connection to Philo

300
00:13:51,787 --> 00:13:53,093
maybe we should look elsewhere?

301
00:13:53,180 --> 00:13:55,747
No. No, Philo is all we have.

302
00:13:55,835 --> 00:13:58,098
If we don't bust him,
he busts us.

303
00:13:58,185 --> 00:14:00,230
Let's dig deep into Gary Philo

304
00:14:00,317 --> 00:14:01,884
see if we can get any leverage

305
00:14:01,971 --> 00:14:04,278
medical records,
bank accounts, legal filings.

306
00:14:04,365 --> 00:14:05,801
With zero solid evidence

307
00:14:05,888 --> 00:14:07,281
we're never gonna get
warrants for all that.

308
00:14:07,368 --> 00:14:09,718
See if you can call in
any favors. Improvise.

309
00:14:20,033 --> 00:14:22,122
[male #2]
'"Bauer" "mercer drive". yeah.'

310
00:14:22,209 --> 00:14:23,950
We deliver to them.
What's this about?

311
00:14:24,037 --> 00:14:26,082
Did you deliver
to them yesterday?

312
00:14:28,998 --> 00:14:31,087
[male #2]
'"Bauer," "Bauer"'

313
00:14:32,349 --> 00:14:34,438
Nope. No order.

314
00:14:34,525 --> 00:14:36,963
Somebody there
called you at 12:07.

315
00:14:37,050 --> 00:14:39,487
They didn't order anything.
What's this about?

316
00:14:39,574 --> 00:14:42,751
The woman who made
the call has been murdered.

317
00:14:42,838 --> 00:14:45,101
- Oh.
- Manny. What's wrong?

318
00:14:45,188 --> 00:14:46,973
I'll tell you later, sis.

319
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:48,061
So.

320
00:14:48,148 --> 00:14:49,889
Manny, tell me.
What-what is it?

321
00:14:49,976 --> 00:14:52,543
There's been a murder.

322
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:53,849
Oh, my God.

323
00:14:53,936 --> 00:14:55,111
Relax, Lila.

324
00:14:55,198 --> 00:14:56,765
- This a family business?
- Yes.

325
00:14:56,852 --> 00:14:58,898
That's nice. Family's nice.

326
00:14:58,985 --> 00:15:01,509
Does your little
brother James work here also?

327
00:15:01,596 --> 00:15:02,684
Yes, he does.

328
00:15:02,771 --> 00:15:03,772
Is he here now?

329
00:15:03,859 --> 00:15:05,948
No, he's not.

330
00:15:07,341 --> 00:15:08,559
[Jane]
'Lila?'

331
00:15:08,646 --> 00:15:09,865
When James came
back from the army

332
00:15:09,952 --> 00:15:11,040
he was a different
man wasn't he?

333
00:15:13,782 --> 00:15:15,827
Troubled.

334
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:17,742
How do you..

335
00:15:18,569 --> 00:15:19,962
Hey, uh, Lila.

336
00:15:21,659 --> 00:15:22,922
- Are you okay?
- She's fine, it's all good.

337
00:15:28,014 --> 00:15:29,667
Your brother's full name
is James Barca?

338
00:15:31,234 --> 00:15:32,844
When did you last
see or hear from him?

339
00:15:33,628 --> 00:15:36,022
Uh, yesterday.

340
00:15:36,109 --> 00:15:39,895
He left here at about 12:30,
but nothing since then.

341
00:15:39,982 --> 00:15:42,550
This thing is not him. No way.

342
00:15:44,465 --> 00:15:46,771
What do you think, Lila?

343
00:15:47,381 --> 00:15:49,426
N-no.

344
00:15:49,513 --> 00:15:51,515
There's no way it's him.

345
00:15:51,602 --> 00:15:53,604
But?

346
00:15:54,605 --> 00:15:56,607
It's-it's like you said.

347
00:15:56,694 --> 00:15:59,741
He came back from the army
two years ago, and..

348
00:15:59,828 --> 00:16:02,178
...ever since then,
he's been angry and..

349
00:16:03,397 --> 00:16:06,095
...sad and-and violent
sometimes.

350
00:16:06,182 --> 00:16:08,706
Barroom brawls. No big deal.

351
00:16:08,793 --> 00:16:10,012
But he has blackouts and stuff.

352
00:16:11,492 --> 00:16:13,102
And he quit going
to the doctors

353
00:16:13,189 --> 00:16:14,930
and he self-medicates,
and that only makes it worse--

354
00:16:15,017 --> 00:16:17,106
Lila. He's our brother.

355
00:16:18,455 --> 00:16:20,283
And I love him so much, but he--

356
00:16:22,590 --> 00:16:24,766
Manny, what if he did this?

357
00:16:24,853 --> 00:16:26,594
This is not him. Okay?

358
00:16:29,162 --> 00:16:30,946
A car registered
to a James Barca

359
00:16:31,033 --> 00:16:33,340
just got a parking
ticket this morning

360
00:16:33,427 --> 00:16:35,820
half a block
from the Bauer house.

361
00:16:35,907 --> 00:16:37,997
Oh, my God.

362
00:16:41,391 --> 00:16:43,524
Do you have any idea
where your brother might be?

363
00:16:43,611 --> 00:16:46,048
No. I'm done talking.

364
00:16:47,745 --> 00:16:50,531
If James Barca's
car is at Kati's

365
00:16:50,618 --> 00:16:53,577
then he went to
the murder scene in Kati's car

366
00:16:53,664 --> 00:16:55,014
which was left there.

367
00:16:55,101 --> 00:16:57,581
The question is,
how did he leave?

368
00:17:01,542 --> 00:17:03,587
Either he had an accomplice
with another vehicle

369
00:17:03,674 --> 00:17:04,675
or he left on foot.

370
00:17:06,155 --> 00:17:08,549
Or he's still here.

371
00:17:09,593 --> 00:17:12,074
James!

372
00:17:12,161 --> 00:17:14,642
Come out, come out,
wherever you are!

373
00:17:17,384 --> 00:17:19,821
So, Jane, I need
to ask you a question.

374
00:17:19,908 --> 00:17:22,302
Mm.

375
00:17:24,347 --> 00:17:26,697
You still truly
believe Red John is dead?

376
00:17:30,266 --> 00:17:32,747
I don't truly
believe anything.

377
00:17:32,834 --> 00:17:35,880
That's not answering
the question.

378
00:17:35,967 --> 00:17:38,318
Is this an official inquiry?

379
00:17:38,405 --> 00:17:41,103
Should I have
a lawyer present?

380
00:17:41,190 --> 00:17:43,714
Not unless
you've done something wrong.

381
00:17:43,801 --> 00:17:46,195
Have you?

382
00:17:58,816 --> 00:18:01,080
Don't move. Drop the knife.

383
00:18:01,167 --> 00:18:02,472
No.

384
00:18:02,559 --> 00:18:04,953
I said drop the knife!

385
00:18:05,040 --> 00:18:06,955
Stop moving, or I'll shoot.

386
00:18:07,042 --> 00:18:08,739
James, listen, you keep coming

387
00:18:08,826 --> 00:18:10,959
she's not gonna shoot her dead
like you want her to.

388
00:18:11,046 --> 00:18:13,179
She's just gonna cripple you.

389
00:18:13,266 --> 00:18:16,269
Let go of the knife, James.
You can kill yourself later.

390
00:18:16,356 --> 00:18:18,662
Right now you gotta clean
this mess up with some grace

391
00:18:18,749 --> 00:18:20,795
some dignity.

392
00:18:20,882 --> 00:18:22,188
Give your family
a proper goodbye.

393
00:18:22,275 --> 00:18:24,842
You go out like a soldier.

394
00:18:33,634 --> 00:18:37,159
[Wayne]
'Matilda Cruz
loved Shakespeare.'

395
00:18:37,246 --> 00:18:39,466
What's your favorite
Shakespeare play, Gary?

396
00:18:42,382 --> 00:18:43,383
You will learn in time..

397
00:18:44,601 --> 00:18:46,560
...Wayne..

398
00:18:46,647 --> 00:18:49,998
...to call me "Mr. Philo"..

399
00:18:50,085 --> 00:18:52,131
...as for Shakespeare..

400
00:18:52,218 --> 00:18:53,741
...it's a bunch of English
fruits in tights

401
00:18:53,828 --> 00:18:55,090
running around going..

402
00:18:55,177 --> 00:18:57,701
..."blah-blah,
blah-blah, blah-blah."

403
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:01,270
Give me a break.

404
00:19:01,357 --> 00:19:03,185
Zero interest, okay?

405
00:19:04,926 --> 00:19:09,670
The idea of me meeting anyone
in a Shakespeare chat room

406
00:19:09,757 --> 00:19:11,367
is beyond ludicrous.

407
00:19:16,807 --> 00:19:19,158
[crickets chirping]

408
00:19:22,639 --> 00:19:24,772
[Darcy]
'Kati called up and
ordered a pizza from you?'

409
00:19:24,859 --> 00:19:26,034
Yes, she did, ma'am.

410
00:19:26,121 --> 00:19:27,731
'And then you went
to her house?'

411
00:19:27,818 --> 00:19:29,994
Yes, ma'am, I did.

412
00:19:30,081 --> 00:19:32,432
And then what happened?

413
00:19:34,260 --> 00:19:35,435
I don't know.

414
00:19:35,522 --> 00:19:38,655
Yeah. Sure.

415
00:19:38,742 --> 00:19:41,789
Look, James, you've got
Kati's blood all over your body.

416
00:19:43,921 --> 00:19:46,141
We found you holding
the knife that killed her.

417
00:19:46,228 --> 00:19:48,535
It's gonna be your DNA
on the rape kit, isn't it?

418
00:19:48,622 --> 00:19:51,015
I'm not denying what I did,
but I don't remember doing it.

419
00:19:53,192 --> 00:19:55,542
I must have blacked out.

420
00:19:55,629 --> 00:19:58,893
When I woke up and she was dead,
and I was covered in her blood

421
00:19:58,980 --> 00:20:00,634
I was crazy scared.

422
00:20:02,984 --> 00:20:04,551
I ran into the woods,
I found a hole

423
00:20:04,638 --> 00:20:06,292
and I crawled in it.

424
00:20:06,379 --> 00:20:08,163
And I thought I'd been hiding
in it for days and days

425
00:20:08,250 --> 00:20:09,469
but this
is only tomorrow, right?

426
00:20:13,516 --> 00:20:15,170
Weird.

427
00:20:15,257 --> 00:20:17,607
Every man and his dog
asks you this question, I bet

428
00:20:17,694 --> 00:20:19,609
how many people did you kill
when you were a soldier?

429
00:20:25,789 --> 00:20:26,790
Eight.

430
00:20:26,877 --> 00:20:28,052
So Kati makes nine?

431
00:20:28,139 --> 00:20:29,532
No. Yes.

432
00:20:31,012 --> 00:20:32,100
No.

433
00:20:32,187 --> 00:20:33,449
No, yes, no.

434
00:20:36,322 --> 00:20:40,151
You went to her house
without a pizza.

435
00:20:40,239 --> 00:20:42,763
That means you must always have
planned to kill her, right?

436
00:20:44,547 --> 00:20:46,201
You're on a mission?

437
00:20:48,334 --> 00:20:50,249
Yes, that's right.

438
00:20:51,598 --> 00:20:53,077
I was on a mission.

439
00:20:53,164 --> 00:20:54,253
Hmm.

440
00:20:57,168 --> 00:20:58,779
You're going down, mister.

441
00:20:58,866 --> 00:21:00,650
You hear me?
You're going down.

442
00:21:00,737 --> 00:21:04,350
I know that, sir.
Thank you.

443
00:21:04,437 --> 00:21:06,439
When did you establish
Phiz Systems, Mr. Philo?

444
00:21:06,526 --> 00:21:08,919
[cell phone rings]

445
00:21:10,312 --> 00:21:11,748
Hello, Jane.

446
00:21:11,835 --> 00:21:13,141
Philo confess?

447
00:21:13,228 --> 00:21:14,969
'Nope. He's tight as a drum.'

448
00:21:15,056 --> 00:21:16,449
He won't even admit
to liking Shakespeare.

449
00:21:17,406 --> 00:21:18,886
It's so frustrating

450
00:21:18,973 --> 00:21:21,454
because it seems like
he's gonna crack at any moment.

451
00:21:21,541 --> 00:21:24,021
If we could just
find his weak spot.

452
00:21:24,108 --> 00:21:26,415
He's a know-all.
Loves to be right.

453
00:21:26,502 --> 00:21:27,938
That's where to hit him.

454
00:21:28,025 --> 00:21:29,288
'Okay.'

455
00:21:29,375 --> 00:21:30,724
'And how are things
going there?'

456
00:21:30,811 --> 00:21:32,987
Well, we have
a suspect in custody.

457
00:21:33,074 --> 00:21:34,641
Looks like our man.

458
00:21:34,728 --> 00:21:37,774
I'm not so sure
he is, though.

459
00:21:37,861 --> 00:21:40,777
Same old Jane.
And how's Darcy?

460
00:21:40,864 --> 00:21:42,866
Oh, she's right here, if you
want to ask her directly.

461
00:21:42,953 --> 00:21:45,826
Mm, no, thanks. But send her
my best wishes.

462
00:21:45,913 --> 00:21:47,784
Okay. Bye.

463
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:51,832
Lisbon sends her love.

464
00:21:51,919 --> 00:21:53,355
Oh. I doubt that.

465
00:21:53,442 --> 00:21:54,487
Oh?

466
00:21:54,574 --> 00:21:55,749
She doesn't like me very much.

467
00:21:55,836 --> 00:21:56,967
Why would you say that?

468
00:21:57,054 --> 00:21:58,229
Oh, come on. Enough about that.

469
00:21:58,317 --> 00:21:59,883
Why don't you think
this is our man?

470
00:21:59,970 --> 00:22:02,103
Ah, it's just a feeling.

471
00:22:02,190 --> 00:22:04,801
Can't really put
my finger on it.

472
00:22:04,888 --> 00:22:07,935
- I disagree.
- Hmm. Okay.

473
00:22:08,022 --> 00:22:09,502
Case closed, then.

474
00:22:09,589 --> 00:22:11,242
Lovely working with you.

475
00:22:11,330 --> 00:22:12,548
Wait a minute.
The case isn't closed.

476
00:22:12,635 --> 00:22:14,115
We have to wait for
the blood and DNA.

477
00:22:14,202 --> 00:22:15,551
We've gotta
get this right.

478
00:22:15,638 --> 00:22:18,119
Lieutenant governor's
watching it very closely.

479
00:22:20,948 --> 00:22:21,992
The lieutenant governor
doesn't give

480
00:22:22,079 --> 00:22:23,559
a damn about this case.

481
00:22:23,646 --> 00:22:25,344
Quit fooling.
What's the real story here?

482
00:22:25,431 --> 00:22:29,130
Why do you want me
on this case so badly?

483
00:22:29,217 --> 00:22:33,221
Okay. You're right. I lied.

484
00:22:33,308 --> 00:22:38,008
I asked for you on the case
because I need your help.

485
00:22:38,095 --> 00:22:39,227
My job's in jeopardy.

486
00:22:39,314 --> 00:22:41,577
It's...you know,
it's office politics.

487
00:22:41,664 --> 00:22:42,926
I need a win.

488
00:22:43,013 --> 00:22:44,711
But you have a win.

489
00:22:44,798 --> 00:22:46,930
You're confident
James Barca's guilty?

490
00:22:47,017 --> 00:22:48,062
But you're not.

491
00:22:48,149 --> 00:22:49,933
And what if you're right?

492
00:22:51,935 --> 00:22:54,547
Hey. Help me.
Come on. Please.

493
00:22:55,896 --> 00:22:58,159
Stay.

494
00:22:58,246 --> 00:23:00,640
If I stay and I'm right..

495
00:23:00,727 --> 00:23:02,685
...we run this case my way.

496
00:23:02,772 --> 00:23:03,730
I'm here to learn.

497
00:23:03,817 --> 00:23:06,428
[chuckles]

498
00:23:06,515 --> 00:23:08,648
Then I'll stay.

499
00:23:21,138 --> 00:23:23,663
Do you recognize
this item?

500
00:23:23,750 --> 00:23:25,360
No.

501
00:23:25,447 --> 00:23:27,884
Are you sure?

502
00:23:27,971 --> 00:23:29,016
Take a closer look.

503
00:23:31,192 --> 00:23:33,107
- I'm sure.
- No.

504
00:23:33,194 --> 00:23:35,109
Take a really good look.

505
00:23:37,328 --> 00:23:39,722
[sighs]

506
00:23:39,809 --> 00:23:41,724
It's a beautiful
object, isn't it?

507
00:23:41,811 --> 00:23:44,423
- Hmm. Suppose so.
- Yeah.

508
00:23:44,510 --> 00:23:47,730
Restful to look at.

509
00:23:47,817 --> 00:23:49,645
Very..

510
00:23:49,732 --> 00:23:52,474
...restful.

511
00:23:53,344 --> 00:23:55,912
[sniggers]

512
00:23:55,999 --> 00:23:58,262
Wait.

513
00:23:58,349 --> 00:24:01,135
Are you really
trying to hypnotizeme?

514
00:24:01,222 --> 00:24:02,963
- No.
- Yes, you are.

515
00:24:03,050 --> 00:24:04,965
Just checking to see
if you recognize that.

516
00:24:05,052 --> 00:24:08,098
No.

517
00:24:08,185 --> 00:24:11,014
[Gary]
'You were. You were trying
to hypnotize me.'

518
00:24:11,101 --> 00:24:13,016
No. No, I wasn't.

519
00:24:15,497 --> 00:24:16,411
[Susan]
'Before we were so rudely'

520
00:24:16,498 --> 00:24:18,979
'interrupted by James Barca'

521
00:24:19,066 --> 00:24:21,982
I was asking you
about Red John.

522
00:24:22,069 --> 00:24:25,725
Now? When I have
eggs coming?

523
00:24:25,812 --> 00:24:27,683
Well, I can't help it.
I'm an FBI agent.

524
00:24:27,770 --> 00:24:29,642
I like to get at the truth.

525
00:24:29,729 --> 00:24:30,773
Hmm.

526
00:24:30,860 --> 00:24:33,297
I tell you what.

527
00:24:33,384 --> 00:24:35,822
How about I do an old-school
psychic reading on you

528
00:24:35,909 --> 00:24:37,911
and then we'll talk Red John?

529
00:24:37,998 --> 00:24:39,956
You wanna do your act?

530
00:24:40,043 --> 00:24:42,829
Mm, what can I say?
I'm a showman.

531
00:24:42,916 --> 00:24:45,353
Okay.

532
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:47,398
Do your reading
and then we'll talk.

533
00:24:47,486 --> 00:24:52,229
You have to be
completely honest with me.

534
00:24:52,316 --> 00:24:54,971
Of course.

535
00:24:56,103 --> 00:24:57,713
Happy childhood?

536
00:24:57,800 --> 00:25:00,063
Yes.

537
00:25:00,150 --> 00:25:02,065
Who was your
favorite, mom or dad?

538
00:25:02,152 --> 00:25:03,371
Dad.

539
00:25:03,458 --> 00:25:05,808
Mom showed weakness,
Dad was stronger?

540
00:25:08,071 --> 00:25:09,072
Yeah, I guess.

541
00:25:09,159 --> 00:25:10,813
Stronger how?

542
00:25:10,900 --> 00:25:14,034
I don't know.
Uh, you know. Life.

543
00:25:14,121 --> 00:25:16,253
No. Something more
specific than that.

544
00:25:16,340 --> 00:25:18,647
Something you..

545
00:25:18,734 --> 00:25:20,301
...can't talk about easily.

546
00:25:24,610 --> 00:25:26,176
You have the
confidence of an only child

547
00:25:26,263 --> 00:25:29,223
yet there's sadness also.

548
00:25:34,402 --> 00:25:37,100
You lost somebody.

549
00:25:37,187 --> 00:25:39,407
Everybody's lost somebody.

550
00:25:39,494 --> 00:25:43,890
Yes, but you lost
a sister or a brother.

551
00:25:43,977 --> 00:25:46,501
- Which?
- Sister.

552
00:25:46,588 --> 00:25:48,198
Drowning. It wasn't
anybody's fault.

553
00:25:48,285 --> 00:25:51,114
You blame yourself,
which you know is crazy.

554
00:25:53,377 --> 00:25:54,814
Oh, wow.

555
00:25:55,858 --> 00:25:57,860
You're good. Huh.

556
00:26:00,515 --> 00:26:03,300
That's a neat skill.

557
00:26:05,085 --> 00:26:07,522
So now let's talk
about Red John.

558
00:26:07,609 --> 00:26:10,307
That's why you're
relentless in your work.

559
00:26:10,394 --> 00:26:12,527
Because you're
relentlessly hard on yourself.

560
00:26:12,614 --> 00:26:15,051
You know, you should
cut yourself some slack.

561
00:26:15,138 --> 00:26:16,487
You'd be a lot happier.

562
00:26:16,575 --> 00:26:17,619
Come on. You don't
know anything--

563
00:26:17,706 --> 00:26:18,925
You're lonely
and you're sad

564
00:26:19,012 --> 00:26:20,491
because you lock
down your emotions

565
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:21,580
which accounts for your

566
00:26:21,667 --> 00:26:23,712
stilted seduction technique.

567
00:26:23,799 --> 00:26:25,322
You're capable
of great passion

568
00:26:25,409 --> 00:26:27,150
if you'd just
let yourself go.

569
00:26:27,237 --> 00:26:28,674
But you won't

570
00:26:28,761 --> 00:26:30,893
not until you redeem yourself

571
00:26:30,980 --> 00:26:33,287
make the wrong things right.

572
00:26:33,374 --> 00:26:34,810
But how can
you hope to do that?

573
00:26:34,897 --> 00:26:37,117
You can't bring
your sister back.

574
00:26:37,204 --> 00:26:39,989
You're on your own.

575
00:26:45,212 --> 00:26:48,041
Deep down inside,
you're a cruel man, aren't you?

576
00:26:56,440 --> 00:26:57,528
Here you go, hon.

577
00:26:57,616 --> 00:26:59,748
[mug clatters]

578
00:27:00,967 --> 00:27:02,838
Ah.

579
00:27:09,758 --> 00:27:12,108
- Susan, wait.
- Seriously, Patrick.

580
00:27:12,195 --> 00:27:13,719
- I'm not interested.
- Oh, never mind that.

581
00:27:13,806 --> 00:27:15,546
I realized what's
bugging me about this case.

582
00:27:18,637 --> 00:27:20,073
Agents.

583
00:27:20,160 --> 00:27:21,465
Have you charged him?

584
00:27:21,552 --> 00:27:22,945
Charged who?

585
00:27:23,032 --> 00:27:25,078
The man you arrested.
Uh, the pizza guy.

586
00:27:25,165 --> 00:27:26,993
Who told you
we'd arrested anyone?

587
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:30,866
Ah. Of course.
Ranger Franklin.

588
00:27:30,953 --> 00:27:32,172
- I was told in confidence.
- Well.

589
00:27:32,259 --> 00:27:33,652
Never mind who
we have in custody.

590
00:27:33,739 --> 00:27:36,785
We need to look
through your wife's stuff.

591
00:27:36,872 --> 00:27:38,439
Now.

592
00:27:38,526 --> 00:27:40,223
This is her room.

593
00:27:40,310 --> 00:27:42,269
Separate bedrooms, huh?

594
00:27:42,356 --> 00:27:44,271
If you tell me what you're
looking for maybe I can help.

595
00:27:44,358 --> 00:27:46,012
Ooh, I doubt
that very much.

596
00:27:46,099 --> 00:27:47,317
[baby crying]

597
00:27:47,404 --> 00:27:48,710
Excuse me.

598
00:27:51,887 --> 00:27:53,454
What are you looking for?

599
00:27:53,541 --> 00:27:55,195
Uh.

600
00:27:55,282 --> 00:27:57,632
Kati wasn't wearing
her wedding ring.

601
00:28:00,243 --> 00:28:01,201
Yes. You're right.

602
00:28:02,811 --> 00:28:04,944
Maybe James Barca took it.

603
00:28:07,163 --> 00:28:09,209
He didn't have it, did he?

604
00:28:12,168 --> 00:28:13,474
Maybe he threw it away.

605
00:28:13,561 --> 00:28:17,347
Possibly. But why?

606
00:28:17,434 --> 00:28:19,567
More likely, she
wasn't wearing her ring

607
00:28:19,654 --> 00:28:20,742
because she left it at home.

608
00:28:20,829 --> 00:28:22,091
Because that's what people do

609
00:28:22,178 --> 00:28:23,527
when they're having an affair.

610
00:28:28,881 --> 00:28:30,186
Were you and Kati
having an affair?

611
00:28:30,273 --> 00:28:31,405
Oh, come on.

612
00:28:31,492 --> 00:28:33,494
No, ma'am,
we were not.

613
00:28:33,581 --> 00:28:36,149
Oh, James, don't deny
yourselves the truth.

614
00:28:36,236 --> 00:28:39,630
You and Kati were
in love. Or am I wrong?

615
00:28:39,718 --> 00:28:42,721
Was it just a little...fun?
A little lust?

616
00:28:48,901 --> 00:28:50,119
No.

617
00:28:52,034 --> 00:28:55,734
I loved her.
And she loved me.

618
00:28:55,821 --> 00:28:58,127
She was the best thing
that ever happened to me.

619
00:28:58,214 --> 00:28:59,781
Why didn't you
tell us before?

620
00:28:59,868 --> 00:29:02,131
Tell you that
she was cheating? What for?

621
00:29:02,218 --> 00:29:04,133
'I don't wanna make it seem
like she was a bad person.'

622
00:29:04,220 --> 00:29:05,395
'Give her family more grief.'

623
00:29:05,482 --> 00:29:07,397
Well, that's
very considerate of you.

624
00:29:09,617 --> 00:29:12,098
How long were you together?

625
00:29:12,185 --> 00:29:13,795
Six months.

626
00:29:15,623 --> 00:29:18,060
I delivered a pizza one time,
and we got to talking.

627
00:29:18,147 --> 00:29:20,976
She was..

628
00:29:21,063 --> 00:29:24,153
...lonely, and..

629
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:26,286
...I don't know,
we got along.

630
00:29:28,114 --> 00:29:31,770
She said we must have been
married in a previous life.

631
00:29:31,857 --> 00:29:33,641
Does anyone else
know about the relationship?

632
00:29:33,728 --> 00:29:36,252
No.

633
00:29:36,339 --> 00:29:38,167
Look, James, if you
can prove you were lovers

634
00:29:38,254 --> 00:29:40,082
you might be able
to avoid the death penalty.

635
00:29:40,169 --> 00:29:41,997
I don't want to avoid it.

636
00:29:43,738 --> 00:29:45,653
I killed her.

637
00:29:46,915 --> 00:29:50,440
See, thing is, James.

638
00:29:50,527 --> 00:29:53,095
I'm about to..

639
00:29:53,182 --> 00:29:55,532
...80% sure that
you didn't kill her.

640
00:29:59,710 --> 00:30:02,409
I wish that were true.

641
00:30:02,496 --> 00:30:03,671
We'll see.

642
00:30:12,158 --> 00:30:14,247
[birds chirping]

643
00:30:14,334 --> 00:30:16,815
I found this
in your wife's bedroom.

644
00:30:16,902 --> 00:30:17,903
Make of it what you will

645
00:30:17,990 --> 00:30:20,644
but Mr. Barca is innocent.

646
00:30:20,731 --> 00:30:22,864
Your wife's killer
is still out there.

647
00:30:22,951 --> 00:30:24,648
But the man
confessed, didn't he?

648
00:30:24,735 --> 00:30:26,955
Well, he's just
a messed-up kid, that's all.

649
00:30:27,042 --> 00:30:29,523
I could kick him myself.

650
00:30:29,610 --> 00:30:31,525
The truth was right there.

651
00:30:31,612 --> 00:30:35,181
I missed it
by a hair. A hair.

652
00:30:35,268 --> 00:30:36,660
Brush aside the distractions

653
00:30:36,747 --> 00:30:39,054
and it's just so obvious.

654
00:30:39,141 --> 00:30:40,621
[Sandy]
'What is?
I don't follow you.'

655
00:30:40,708 --> 00:30:41,796
No need. No need.

656
00:30:41,883 --> 00:30:43,276
Leave it to us professionals.

657
00:30:43,363 --> 00:30:45,974
And rest assured, the true
culprit will pay dearly.

658
00:30:48,150 --> 00:30:49,804
Excuse me.

659
00:30:49,891 --> 00:30:52,111
"The true culprit
will pay dearly"?

660
00:30:52,198 --> 00:30:54,243
That a little
too theatrical for you?

661
00:30:54,330 --> 00:30:55,331
Where are we going now?

662
00:30:55,418 --> 00:30:56,376
Right now we're going back

663
00:30:56,463 --> 00:30:57,812
to the forest service office.

664
00:30:57,899 --> 00:30:59,683
- To do what?
- To release James Barca.

665
00:30:59,770 --> 00:31:01,816
And get a search warrant
for his brother Manny.

666
00:31:01,903 --> 00:31:04,166
Manny Barca is your culprit?
What's his motive?

667
00:31:04,253 --> 00:31:07,430
Well, who knows? Maybe he was
in love with Kati too.

668
00:31:07,517 --> 00:31:08,692
Come on. That's not enough.

669
00:31:08,779 --> 00:31:11,043
Sure, it is.

670
00:31:12,174 --> 00:31:14,960
Jane, it's not enough.

671
00:31:15,047 --> 00:31:17,136
Deal was we run
this case my way.

672
00:31:17,223 --> 00:31:19,486
Well, the deal's off.

673
00:31:19,573 --> 00:31:20,661
We're charging
James Barca as soon as

674
00:31:20,748 --> 00:31:21,836
the blood work gets back.

675
00:31:21,923 --> 00:31:23,882
I'm sick and tired
of your antics.

676
00:31:23,969 --> 00:31:25,100
Kati Bauer deserves
better than these

677
00:31:25,187 --> 00:31:26,841
stupid games you're playing.

678
00:31:26,928 --> 00:31:28,408
Oh, don't talk
to me about games.

679
00:31:28,495 --> 00:31:30,758
And you don't care
a damn about Kati Bauer.

680
00:31:30,845 --> 00:31:35,806
- Nor is your job in jeopardy.
- Excuse me?

681
00:31:35,894 --> 00:31:38,897
I'm only here so you can
get a good close-up look at me.

682
00:31:38,984 --> 00:31:41,290
Why would I want to get
a good, close-up look at you?

683
00:31:41,377 --> 00:31:43,771
You tell me.
And quit your play-acting.

684
00:31:43,858 --> 00:31:45,773
'Cause you're really
not very good at it.

685
00:31:56,740 --> 00:31:59,221
Red John is alive,
and you know it.

686
00:32:00,657 --> 00:32:02,094
Well, that's a theory.

687
00:32:02,181 --> 00:32:04,183
It's a fact.

688
00:32:04,270 --> 00:32:07,012
And the only question is,
why would you deny it?

689
00:32:07,099 --> 00:32:09,579
Why would you try
and throw me off the trail?

690
00:32:12,756 --> 00:32:14,889
I realized something.
You're not hunting Red John.

691
00:32:14,976 --> 00:32:16,282
You're protecting him
and that's why you..

692
00:32:16,369 --> 00:32:17,326
...claim he's dead.

693
00:32:17,413 --> 00:32:18,545
That's absurd.

694
00:32:18,632 --> 00:32:21,113
What other conclusion
could I come to?

695
00:32:21,200 --> 00:32:23,419
If you're enemies,
why hasn't he killed you?

696
00:32:23,506 --> 00:32:24,855
Well..

697
00:32:24,943 --> 00:32:26,422
Why hasn't he killed
the people you work with?

698
00:32:26,509 --> 00:32:28,685
I don't know.

699
00:32:28,772 --> 00:32:30,122
Well, if Red John is dead

700
00:32:30,209 --> 00:32:32,298
why are the people
that cross him still dying?

701
00:32:32,385 --> 00:32:33,908
Because he has friends.

702
00:32:33,995 --> 00:32:36,041
He has disciples.

703
00:32:39,566 --> 00:32:40,610
Are you one of them?

704
00:32:40,697 --> 00:32:42,134
Is that why you're
protecting him?

705
00:32:42,221 --> 00:32:45,006
I wasn't protecting him,
I was protecting you.

706
00:32:45,093 --> 00:32:47,008
No, Susan.
You have this all wrong.

707
00:32:47,095 --> 00:32:48,531
Patrick, how can I
believe a word you say?

708
00:32:48,618 --> 00:32:53,362
You won't even admit
that Red John is alive.

709
00:32:55,103 --> 00:32:57,714
Okay. Fine.

710
00:32:59,281 --> 00:33:02,850
I admit that Red John
is still alive.

711
00:33:02,937 --> 00:33:03,938
Why did you deny it?

712
00:33:04,025 --> 00:33:05,026
It was a mistake.

713
00:33:05,113 --> 00:33:06,071
But I am not his friend

714
00:33:06,158 --> 00:33:08,595
nor am I one of his disciples.

715
00:33:08,682 --> 00:33:10,075
I sure hope
you can prove that.

716
00:33:10,162 --> 00:33:12,381
Well, I don't have
to prove anything.

717
00:33:12,468 --> 00:33:14,688
You do.

718
00:33:22,348 --> 00:33:25,612
Well, I guess we have
nothing more to do here.

719
00:33:33,359 --> 00:33:35,361
[Susan]
'Bye, Patrick.'

720
00:33:45,414 --> 00:33:46,546
[Franklin]
'Mr. Jane.'

721
00:33:46,633 --> 00:33:47,982
Ah, Franklin.

722
00:33:48,069 --> 00:33:51,072
Listen, uh, big news.

723
00:33:51,159 --> 00:33:53,770
We're gonna release James Barca
first thing tomorrow morning.

724
00:33:53,857 --> 00:33:55,511
Agent Darcy's
getting a search warrant

725
00:33:55,598 --> 00:33:57,426
for his brother Manny's
house and car.

726
00:33:57,513 --> 00:33:58,732
Holy cow. Why?

727
00:33:58,819 --> 00:34:00,734
No real evidence yet

728
00:34:00,821 --> 00:34:04,216
but we're certain
that Manny is our man.

729
00:34:04,303 --> 00:34:06,740
You wanna help us
execute the search warrants?

730
00:34:06,827 --> 00:34:08,220
- Sure thing.
- Alright.

731
00:34:08,307 --> 00:34:10,918
I'll call you
first thing in the morning.

732
00:34:12,659 --> 00:34:14,617
Hey, Jane.
How's it going?

733
00:34:16,097 --> 00:34:17,925
- I need your help.
- 'My help?'

734
00:34:18,012 --> 00:34:19,666
- What about Darcy?
- 'Uh.'

735
00:34:19,753 --> 00:34:21,276
It didn't work out.
Long story.

736
00:34:21,363 --> 00:34:23,844
Uh, no big deal. I'll tell
you when you get here.

737
00:34:23,931 --> 00:34:25,846
- I'm busy here!
- 'Call me when you're close.'

738
00:34:28,675 --> 00:34:30,329
I have to go. Jane needs me.

739
00:34:30,416 --> 00:34:32,461
You guys are gonna have to
handle this yourselves.

740
00:34:32,548 --> 00:34:35,464
Uh, okay.

741
00:34:40,991 --> 00:34:43,255
[male #3]
'Here's some water.'

742
00:34:43,342 --> 00:34:45,692
Thank you.

743
00:34:54,353 --> 00:34:56,616
Thank you.

744
00:35:08,410 --> 00:35:11,718
Traces of DNA were found
under Matilda's fingernails.

745
00:35:13,720 --> 00:35:15,765
Ah.

746
00:35:15,852 --> 00:35:18,464
Ah, so nowyou're going
to test my DNA

747
00:35:18,551 --> 00:35:22,076
by sampling the saliva
I left on that bottle, huh?

748
00:35:22,163 --> 00:35:23,469
That drink of water's
gonna be pretty expensive.

749
00:35:23,556 --> 00:35:25,079
You're busted.

750
00:35:25,166 --> 00:35:26,167
Do yourself a favor.

751
00:35:26,254 --> 00:35:27,342
Get out ahead of this.

752
00:35:27,429 --> 00:35:29,039
Talk to us.

753
00:35:29,127 --> 00:35:30,650
Well, these days I imagine

754
00:35:30,737 --> 00:35:33,653
an intelligent killer
will be very careful

755
00:35:33,740 --> 00:35:36,656
not to leave DNA
on his or her victim.

756
00:35:36,743 --> 00:35:38,310
Everybody makes mistakes.

757
00:35:38,397 --> 00:35:40,007
[chuckles]

758
00:35:41,704 --> 00:35:43,489
Not the killer
of Matilda Cruz.

759
00:35:46,535 --> 00:35:49,756
Sweetheart, if you ever
want a sample of my DNA

760
00:35:49,843 --> 00:35:52,193
all you gotta do is ask.

761
00:35:57,459 --> 00:36:00,027
[instrumental music]

762
00:36:06,599 --> 00:36:08,557
[car honks]

763
00:36:13,127 --> 00:36:15,347
Okay. What happened
with Darcy?

764
00:36:15,434 --> 00:36:17,479
Um, you're not gonna
ask about the case?

765
00:36:17,566 --> 00:36:18,567
Is it under control?

766
00:36:18,654 --> 00:36:19,873
Yes. I missed you.

767
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:21,744
- I missed you, too.
- Hmm.

768
00:36:21,831 --> 00:36:23,833
- So what happened?
- We fell out.

769
00:36:23,920 --> 00:36:26,184
- Did you crack the Philo case?
- No, not yet.

770
00:36:26,271 --> 00:36:27,489
- That's it? You fell out?
- Well.

771
00:36:27,576 --> 00:36:31,319
You don't think
I'd tell you? Can we go?

772
00:36:31,406 --> 00:36:33,756
[engine revs]

773
00:36:39,936 --> 00:36:42,983
Now we wait.

774
00:36:43,070 --> 00:36:45,420
Tell me what
happened with Darcy.

775
00:36:45,507 --> 00:36:46,682
Hmm.

776
00:36:51,296 --> 00:36:53,036
[switch clicks]

777
00:36:54,951 --> 00:36:56,518
I'm still here.

778
00:36:56,605 --> 00:36:58,738
Yes. I know.

779
00:36:58,825 --> 00:37:01,654
Please, Jane.
Just tell me what happened.

780
00:37:01,741 --> 00:37:03,612
Okay.

781
00:37:03,699 --> 00:37:05,527
I admitted that
Red John is still alive

782
00:37:05,614 --> 00:37:07,312
just like you wanted me to

783
00:37:07,399 --> 00:37:10,228
and she accused me
of working for him.

784
00:37:10,315 --> 00:37:11,577
What?

785
00:37:11,664 --> 00:37:13,231
She thinks I'm one of
Red John's disciples.

786
00:37:13,318 --> 00:37:15,450
The FBI thinks you're
working with Red John?

787
00:37:15,537 --> 00:37:16,669
Don't look
so serious.

788
00:37:16,756 --> 00:37:18,453
- It's a joke.
- What did you tell her?

789
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:20,412
I told her to go
ahead and prove it.

790
00:37:20,499 --> 00:37:22,544
- Oh, my god.
- Well, news flash.

791
00:37:22,631 --> 00:37:23,937
I don't work for Red John

792
00:37:24,024 --> 00:37:25,634
so she's wasting her time.

793
00:37:25,721 --> 00:37:28,071
- What did Darcy use as proof?
- Well, sh...nothing.

794
00:37:28,158 --> 00:37:30,944
How could she have any proof?

795
00:37:31,031 --> 00:37:32,815
I can't believe you
haven't cracked Philo yet.

796
00:37:32,902 --> 00:37:35,296
I mean, you guys just can't
manage without me, can you?

797
00:37:35,383 --> 00:37:37,472
Don't try and divert
me with insults!

798
00:37:37,559 --> 00:37:39,082
Okay, what did she say
she was gonna do?

799
00:37:39,169 --> 00:37:40,693
- She didn't.
- And you didn't ask her?

800
00:37:40,780 --> 00:37:42,260
Ah! Look.

801
00:37:57,231 --> 00:37:58,580
[Jane]
Excuse me.

802
00:37:58,667 --> 00:38:00,278
You're both under arrest!

803
00:38:00,365 --> 00:38:02,932
I'm not sure why, but hey.

804
00:38:04,804 --> 00:38:06,458
[clattering]

805
00:38:08,286 --> 00:38:10,505
You knew Kati was having
an affair with James Barca

806
00:38:10,592 --> 00:38:12,333
but you didn't
tell Sandy because, uh..

807
00:38:12,420 --> 00:38:13,900
...he'd want a divorce.

808
00:38:13,987 --> 00:38:16,555
And in California,
that means that Kati would get

809
00:38:16,642 --> 00:38:20,385
half the assets and joint
custody of Megan, like a reward.

810
00:38:20,472 --> 00:38:22,778
Exactly. A reward
for being a slut.

811
00:38:22,865 --> 00:38:24,693
Hon, I thought
we agreed not to talk

812
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:25,868
till Josh Wagner gets here.

813
00:38:25,955 --> 00:38:27,914
Alright.

814
00:38:28,001 --> 00:38:29,829
When you saw Kati
preparing that picnic lunch

815
00:38:29,916 --> 00:38:32,310
you knew it was for
her regular date with James.

816
00:38:32,397 --> 00:38:34,224
She made potato salad
right in front of me.

817
00:38:34,312 --> 00:38:35,530
As brazen as can be.

818
00:38:35,617 --> 00:38:37,271
And you used
your insulinneedle

819
00:38:37,358 --> 00:38:39,491
to inject a
powerful sedative

820
00:38:39,578 --> 00:38:41,493
horse tranquilizer, probably

821
00:38:41,580 --> 00:38:44,931
into all of the wine bottles of that wine rack.

822
00:38:45,018 --> 00:38:47,977
So that whichever bottle Kati took

823
00:38:48,064 --> 00:38:49,892
your plan would still work.

824
00:38:59,641 --> 00:39:01,339
[Shirley] It was a good plan.

825
00:39:09,129 --> 00:39:12,001
It was a very good plan.

826
00:39:12,088 --> 00:39:14,221
Then why are we here?

827
00:39:14,308 --> 00:39:16,745
Those tiny holes you left

828
00:39:16,832 --> 00:39:20,619
in the wine bottles on the wine
rack that was your downfall.

829
00:39:20,706 --> 00:39:22,490
That, and the shard
of glass that you left

830
00:39:22,577 --> 00:39:24,927
up at the picnic site.

831
00:39:25,014 --> 00:39:26,364
All I needed to do was plant

832
00:39:26,451 --> 00:39:30,846
the word "hairbrush"
in your subconscious.

833
00:39:30,933 --> 00:39:33,283
And bait a trap
with a fake story

834
00:39:33,371 --> 00:39:36,112
to be passed on
by chatty Cathy here.

835
00:39:36,199 --> 00:39:38,550
Whoa. Hey, look.

836
00:39:38,637 --> 00:39:39,638
I had no idea
that I was passing on a--

837
00:39:39,725 --> 00:39:40,682
I was just teasing.

838
00:39:40,769 --> 00:39:42,554
Relax. It's fine.

839
00:39:42,641 --> 00:39:43,946
You did very well.
You did a great job.

840
00:39:44,033 --> 00:39:45,426
In fact, you cracked
the case for us.

841
00:39:45,513 --> 00:39:48,081
And that is exactly
what my report will reflect.

842
00:39:48,168 --> 00:39:50,170
Congratulations.

843
00:39:50,257 --> 00:39:53,303
Well, that's...thank you.

844
00:39:53,391 --> 00:39:54,696
Well, think nothing of it.

845
00:39:54,783 --> 00:39:56,524
You know, your call.

846
00:39:56,611 --> 00:39:58,744
But perhaps you might wanna
release James Barca now.

847
00:39:58,831 --> 00:40:00,398
I'm sure his family
would appreciate it.

848
00:40:02,182 --> 00:40:05,141
- James.
- Oh, my goodness, brother.

849
00:40:05,228 --> 00:40:08,101
Oh! James!
Are you okay?

850
00:40:08,188 --> 00:40:10,712
That's nice.
You did good.

851
00:40:10,799 --> 00:40:12,105
Hmm.

852
00:40:12,192 --> 00:40:14,629
I'll be right back.

853
00:40:14,716 --> 00:40:16,892
Oh. Time to go.

854
00:40:16,979 --> 00:40:20,026
Nope. Uh, um, okay.

855
00:40:20,113 --> 00:40:22,507
Thank you.

856
00:40:22,594 --> 00:40:23,986
Yep.

857
00:40:24,073 --> 00:40:25,118
Thank you.

858
00:40:25,205 --> 00:40:26,598
Yep.

859
00:40:28,382 --> 00:40:31,341
Agent Darcy.
Come on in.

860
00:40:31,429 --> 00:40:33,387
Congratulations.
Case solved.

861
00:40:33,474 --> 00:40:34,954
Although I understand

862
00:40:35,041 --> 00:40:37,173
Jane may have irritated you
somewhat with his antics.

863
00:40:37,260 --> 00:40:38,958
Hey, all's well
that ends well, though, right?

864
00:40:39,045 --> 00:40:42,788
No. We have a problem.

865
00:40:42,875 --> 00:40:44,529
Oh?

866
00:40:44,616 --> 00:40:47,662
These past two days have
confirmed something for me.

867
00:40:47,749 --> 00:40:48,924
I think Patrick Jane
is a cunning

868
00:40:49,011 --> 00:40:50,796
manipulative sociopath.

869
00:40:50,883 --> 00:40:53,755
Oh.

870
00:40:53,842 --> 00:40:57,367
I think he's working
hand-in-hand with Red John.

871
00:40:57,455 --> 00:40:59,195
Really?

872
00:40:59,282 --> 00:41:01,371
If it's true,
we're taking him down.

873
00:41:01,459 --> 00:41:03,243
And you can help,
or you can go down with him.

874
00:41:03,330 --> 00:41:04,462
I'm sorry to be
so blunt

875
00:41:04,549 --> 00:41:06,855
but that's
the FBI perspective.

876
00:41:08,596 --> 00:41:10,468
You deserve full disclosure.

877
00:41:13,601 --> 00:41:15,777
Yes, I see.

878
00:41:17,823 --> 00:41:20,347
Well, you can count
on my full cooperation.

879
00:41:22,697 --> 00:41:25,265
Sorry to inconvenience
you, Mr. Philo.

880
00:41:25,352 --> 00:41:26,875
I'll sign you out
downstairs.

881
00:41:26,962 --> 00:41:29,835
You start thinking
about a new career, Wayne.

882
00:41:29,922 --> 00:41:33,839
'Cause this one's
all over for you.

883
00:41:33,926 --> 00:41:38,713
Ah. Well met. Well met.

884
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:42,543
Uh, I was just, uh,
telling your hapless friend here

885
00:41:42,630 --> 00:41:47,026
I am gonna bury
you morons in lawsuits.

886
00:41:47,113 --> 00:41:48,418
You may think
you've won this one

887
00:41:48,506 --> 00:41:50,551
but look at
your hands, Mr. Philo.

888
00:41:50,638 --> 00:41:53,598
They're stained red
with Matilda's blood.

889
00:41:53,685 --> 00:41:54,947
Will all great
"Neptune's waters"

890
00:41:55,034 --> 00:41:57,471
wash that blood
clean from your hand?

891
00:41:57,558 --> 00:41:59,342
No.

892
00:41:59,429 --> 00:42:03,172
It's "Oceans."
"Neptune's oceans."

893
00:42:03,259 --> 00:42:04,217
You're right.

894
00:42:04,304 --> 00:42:05,305
"Neptune's oceans."

895
00:42:05,392 --> 00:42:08,047
"Macbeth," Act II, scene two.

896
00:42:08,134 --> 00:42:10,745
Shakespeare. You lied
to us, Mr. Philo.

897
00:42:10,832 --> 00:42:12,747
'Why would you lie to us?'

898
00:42:12,834 --> 00:42:15,315
No, no, no,
no, no, no, no.

899
00:42:15,402 --> 00:42:17,796
No, no, no. No.

900
00:42:17,883 --> 00:42:20,755
You need to come talk
to us some more, Gary.

901
00:42:23,715 --> 00:42:24,890
I know. I know.

902
00:42:24,977 --> 00:42:26,108
You don't know
whether to hug me

903
00:42:26,195 --> 00:42:27,196
or to hit me.

904
00:42:27,283 --> 00:42:28,371
- Ugh!
- I--

905
00:42:28,458 --> 00:42:30,852
[instrumental music]

906
00:42:38,512 --> 00:42:40,732
[theme music]

